  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/a_new_panchax_from_manipur_en/",
      "html": "<p>Currently only one species is accepted formally within this species-group, Aplocheilus panchax. It is distributed from China over great parts of India, Burma and Indochina (Thailand, Vietnam, Cambodia, Laos), as well as over Indonesia. Fish from different origins look very different, so it is more than likely that there are many species and not only one.</p><p>Aplocheilus panchax was initially described from Bengal. The new Panchax from Manipur, in northern India, represents such a different form. It is distinguished from typical A. panchax by many red spots on the body, bright orange fins, and a dark band that is shown when the fish feel disturbed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/a_new_panchax_from_manipur_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Manipur form originates from mountain regions, where the temperature easily falls as low as 15°C. The species prefers shallow waters, which can warm up very quickly to 30°C and even more on sunny days.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/a_new_panchax_from_manipur_en/",
      "html": "<p>The new Panchax from Manipur should not be kept too warm throughout the year, as it originates from mountain regions.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/a_new_panchax_from_manipur_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Panchax takes readily any usual fish food, even flakes.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/a_new_panchax_from_manipur_en/",
      "html": "<p>The Panchax is an ideal fish for community tanks, where it lives near the surface. Tankmates should not be too small, otherwise they are eaten.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/a_new_panchax_from_manipur_en/",
      "html": "<p>Males and females can be distinguished easily by the shape of the anal fin, which is shorter and rounded in females. Typical A. panchax from Bengal show polychromatism: within one population there are white-finned and orange-finned males, while the females all look the same. Red spots on the body are never present in typical A. panchax.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/18-toothcarps-killis-and-livebearers/a_new_panchax_from_manipur_en/",
      "html": "<p>Breeding is very easy. A. panchax is an egglaying fish.</p>"
    }
  ],
